All three upstream files fetched successfully via `curl --ssl-no-revoke` (WebFetch was erroring with a harness API error). Sources: `drivers/serial/serial.c` (2468 lines), `drivers/serial/serial_io.c` (331 lines), `include/nuttx/serial/serial.h` (610 lines), `drivers/usbdev/cdcacm.c`, `drivers/serial/Kconfig` — all from `apache/nuttx` master, saved under `C:\Users\flash\AppData\Local\Temp\` (`nuttx_serial.c`, `nuttx_serial_io.c`, `nuttx_serial_h.h`, `cdcacm.c`, `nuttx_serial_kconfig.txt`).

## 1. `uart_putxmitchar()` full-buffer path — confirmed

`drivers/serial/serial.c:256-383`. Buffer-full test is `nexthead != dev->xmit.tail` at line 279; when full and `oktoblock`, lines 292-348:

```c
      else if (oktoblock)
        {
          flags = enter_critical_section();

          if (nexthead != dev->xmit.tail)
            {
              ret = OK;
            }

#ifdef CONFIG_SERIAL_REMOVABLE
          else if (dev->disconnected)
            {
              ret = -ENOTCONN;
            }
#endif
          else
            {
              /* Wait for some characters to be sent from the buffer with
               * the TX interrupt enabled.  When the TX interrupt is enabled,
               * uart_xmitchars() should execute and remove some of the data
               * from the TX buffer.
               ...
               */

#ifdef CONFIG_SERIAL_TXDMA
              uart_dmatxavail(dev);
#endif
              uart_enabletxint(dev);
              ret = nxsem_wait(&dev->xmitsem);
              uart_disabletxint(dev);
            }

          leave_critical_section(flags);
```

Yes: `uart_enabletxint(dev)` then untimed `nxsem_wait(&dev->xmitsem)`.

## 2. Who posts `xmitsem`

`uart_xmitchars()` → `uart_datasent()` → `uart_wakeup(&dev->xmitsem)`. `serial_io.c:125-128`:

```c
  if (nbytes)
    {
      uart_datasent(dev);
    }
```

`serial.c:2309-2318`:

```c
void uart_datasent(FAR uart_dev_t *dev)
{
  uart_poll_notify(dev, 0, CONFIG_SERIAL_NPOLLWAITERS, POLLOUT);
  uart_wakeup(&dev->xmitsem);
}
```

`serial.c:2175-2190` — `uart_wakeup()` posts only up to a value of 1:

```c
static void uart_wakeup(FAR sem_t *sem)
{
  int sval;
  if (nxsem_get_value(sem, &sval) != OK) { return; }
  while (sval++ < 1) { nxsem_post(sem); }
}
```

Note the `if (nbytes)` guard: if zero bytes were moved, **nothing is posted**. The only other posters are `uart_connected(..., false)` (Q8) and `uart_xmitchars_done()` (TXDMA only).

## 3. Call site of `uart_xmitchars()` — TX interrupt only

Header doc, `include/nuttx/serial/serial.h:429-437`:

```
 *  This function is called from the UART interrupt handler when an interrupt
 *  is received indicating that there is more space in the transmit FIFO.
```

Confirmed in your own tree, `esp32p4-openvela-overlay-20260824\nuttx\arch\risc-v\src\esp32p4\espressif\esp_usbserial.c:198-219`, inside `esp_interrupt()` gated on `USB_SERIAL_JTAG_INTR_SERIAL_IN_EMPTY`. No upper-half code path calls it. **If the TX interrupt never fires, `uart_xmitchars()` never runs and `xmitsem` is never posted — the writer blocks forever.**

## 4. The drain loop — confirmed

`serial_io.c:67-105`:

```c
  while (dev->xmit.head != dev->xmit.tail && uart_txready(dev))
    {
      if (dev->ops->sendbuf)
        { ... sent = uart_sendbuf(dev, &dev->xmit.buffer[dev->xmit.tail], sent);
          if (sent > 0) { dev->xmit.tail += sent; nbytes += sent; } }
      else
        {
          uart_send(dev, dev->xmit.buffer[dev->xmit.tail++]);
          nbytes++;
        }

      if (dev->xmit.tail >= dev->xmit.size)
        {
          dev->xmit.tail = 0;
        }
    }
```

If `txready` returns true, it drains the whole ring, then `uart_disabletxint()` when head == tail, then posts via `uart_datasent()` — but **only if `nbytes != 0`**. So a faked `txready` must also actually advance `tail` (i.e. `uart_send()` must consume/discard the byte) for the post to happen. Upstream even flags the failure mode in a comment at `serial_io.c:110-113`: *"Potential bug? If nbytes == 0 && (dev->xmit.head == dev->xmit.tail) && dev->xmitwaiting == true, then disabling the TX interrupt will leave the uart_write() logic waiting to TX to complete with no TX interrupts."*

## 5. Timeout on write — none

There is no `write` in the fops (`serial.c:165-170` has `NULL, /* write */`); the path is `uart_writev()` at `serial.c:1479`. It calls `uart_putxmitchar()` (line 1609), which uses plain `nxsem_wait()` — **no timeout, waits indefinitely**. The only bounded waits in the file are in `uart_tcdrain(dev, cancelable, timeout)` (`serial.c:523`), which uses `nxsem_tickwait(&dev->xmitsem, timeout - elapsed)` and is called with `4 * TICK_PER_SEC` from `uart_close()` (line 890) and `10 * TICK_PER_SEC` from the `TCDRN` ioctl (line 1810).

## 6. `CONFIG_SERIAL_REMOVABLE` behavior — fails fast, with one caveat

Three checks. In `uart_writev()`, up front, so a disconnected device rejects the **entire** write before touching the buffer — `serial.c:1534-1546`:

```c
#ifdef CONFIG_SERIAL_REMOVABLE
  if (dev->disconnected)
    {
      nxmutex_unlock(&dev->xmit.lock);
      return -ENOTCONN;
    }
#endif
```

and for the interrupt/IDLE-context path at `serial.c:1501-1510` (`if (dev->disconnected) { return -ENOTCONN; }`).

In `uart_putxmitchar()`, two places: the pre-wait check quoted in Q1 (lines 326-329), and post-wait, `serial.c:352-361`:

```c
#ifdef CONFIG_SERIAL_REMOVABLE
          /* Check if the removable device was disconnected while we were
           * waiting.
           */

          if (dev->disconnected)
            {
              return -ENOTCONN;
            }
#endif
```

**Caveat:** the `disconnected` test in `uart_putxmitchar()` lives only in the buffer-**full** branch. While the ring still has space, line 279 breaks out at the top and never consults `dev->disconnected`. So bytes keep being accepted into the buffer until it fills — the per-write fast-fail comes from the `uart_writev()` entry check, not from `uart_putxmitchar()`.

## 7. `uart_connected()`

`include/nuttx/serial/serial.h:499-501`:

```c
#ifdef CONFIG_SERIAL_REMOVABLE
void uart_connected(FAR uart_dev_t *dev, bool connected);
#endif
```

State lives in `uart_dev_s` (`serial.h:334-336`): `volatile bool disconnected; /* true: Removable device is not connected */`. Documented as callable from an interrupt handler. `CONFIG_SERIAL_REMOVABLE` is a hidden `bool default n` symbol (`drivers/serial/Kconfig:45`) — no user prompt, it is only `select`ed (e.g. `RPMSG_UART`, `RPMSG_UART_RAW`), so you would `select` it from your board/chip Kconfig.

Reference lower-half usage is `drivers/usbdev/cdcacm.c`: `uart_connected(&priv->serdev, true)` on successful SET_CONFIGURATION (line 1013) and on resume-while-configured (2121); `false` on unconfigure (863), disconnect (2030), and suspend (2083). That is exactly the "USB host present / absent" semantic you want.

## 8. Does disconnect wake blocked writers? — yes

`serial.c:2342-2375`:

```c
void uart_connected(FAR uart_dev_t *dev, bool connected)
{
  irqstate_t flags;

  flags = enter_critical_section();
  sched_lock();
  dev->disconnected = !connected;
  if (!connected)
    {
      /* Notify all poll/select waiters that a hangup occurred */

      poll_notify(dev->fds, CONFIG_SERIAL_NPOLLWAITERS, POLLERR | POLLHUP);

      /* Yes.. wake up all waiting threads.  Each thread should detect the
       * disconnection and return the ENOTCONN error.
       */

      /* Is there a thread waiting for space in xmit.buffer?  */

      uart_wakeup(&dev->xmitsem);

      /* Is there a thread waiting for read data?  */

      uart_wakeup(&dev->recvsem);
    }

  sched_unlock();
  leave_critical_section(flags);
}
```

Already-blocked writers are released and take the post-wait `-ENOTCONN` return from Q6. Note `uart_wakeup()` posts at most to a value of 1, so with multiple blocked writers only one is guaranteed to be released per call.

## Existing upstream TX-timeout / discard mechanism

**There is no TX timeout on the `write()` path and no "discard when nobody is draining" mechanism upstream.** What exists:

- `CONFIG_SERIAL_REMOVABLE` + `uart_connected()` — the only sanctioned "no consumer attached" mechanism, and it is connection-state driven, not timer driven. Nothing polls or times out; a lower half must actively call `uart_connected(dev, false)`.
- `uart_tcdrain()` timeouts — bounded, but only reached via `close()` and `TCDRN`, never from `write()`.
- Buffered-data discard exists in exactly one place, `uart_tcdrain()` at `serial.c:567-577`, and only when already disconnected:

```c
#ifdef CONFIG_SERIAL_REMOVABLE
      if (dev->disconnected)
        {
          dev->xmit.tail = dev->xmit.head;  /* Drop the buffered TX data */
          ret = -ENOTCONN;
        }
      else
#endif
```

- `drivers/serial/Kconfig` has no timeout/discard option for TX; the only `*_TIMEOUT` there is `SERIAL_GDBSTUB_PANIC_TIMEOUT`, unrelated.

## Bearing on your (a) vs (b) choice

Facts, not recommendations:

- **(a) `-ENOTCONN`**: `uart_writev()` returns `-ENOTCONN` for the whole write **only when no byte was queued yet**; if the error hits mid-write, `serial.c:1629-1644` returns the partial byte count instead, so callers see a short write with no errno. `printf`/`syslog` callers generally do not check either, so output is silently lost — which is the intended outcome, but note stdio may set the stream error flag and stop retrying. It also requires the ESP lower half to detect connect/disconnect and drive `uart_connected()` itself, plus `select SERIAL_REMOVABLE`. It is the upstream-sanctioned path.
- **(b) fake `txready` + discard**: must make `uart_send()` actually advance `tail` so `nbytes != 0`, otherwise `uart_datasent()` is skipped (the `if (nbytes)` guard) and the waiter still hangs. Writers see full success. This is a downstream-only pattern with no upstream precedent.

Also worth flagging: your working tree already carries a watchdog-style implementation of (b) — `esp_usbserial.c` has `g_txstalled` / `g_txwdog` cleared in `esp_interrupt()` (lines 210-218), with matching notes at lines 75 and 512-518, and the mechanism is described in `README.md:68-69`.
